course: dictionary
lesson: 3918
title: One small word, many jobs
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: The word from is tiny, but it does many jobs. Most of the time it tells you where something starts, comes from, or is taken away. «Salt from sea spray or from gritted winter roads makes iron rust faster.» Here from shows where the salt comes from.
explain: From can also show a difference or a range. In «take 3 from 10», from tells you to subtract. In «from 9 to 3», it shows when something starts. You can ask yourself: is something starting, leaving, or being taken away here?
explain: Some from words sound informal or formal. «Where you from?» is casual talk and asks someone's home town. «With effect from» and «derived from» are formal, like in an exam or a report. Read the words around from to find the meaning.
explain: When you are stuck, use the context check from earlier lessons. Put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. If it does, you have probably got it right.
fact: From means where something starts or comes from, as in «a letter from Grandma».
fact: From can show what is taken away, as in «take 3 from 10», which means subtract 3 from 10 to get 7.
fact: From can show the start of a time or range, as in «from 9 to 3».
fact: From can show what something is made of, as in «ice is made from water».
fact: «From scratch» means from the very start, and «from the get-go» means from the beginning.
exercise: «Salt from sea spray or from gritted winter roads makes iron rust faster.» In this sentence, what does from show? | The price of the salt | A time when the roads are gritted | *Where the salt comes from | How many grains of salt there are
exercise: In «take 3 from 10», what do you do with the numbers? | Divide 10 by 3 with nothing left over | Add 3 and 10 to get 13 | Multiply 3 by 10 | *Subtract 3 from 10 to get 7
exercise: «Each of them is paid from the price you pay.» Where does the money come from? | *The price you pay | The farmer's field | The shop's windows | A bank in another country
test: «Where you from?» is a casual question. What does it ask? | Whether someone can swim | *Someone's home town or country | What someone wants for lunch | How old someone is
test: What does «from scratch» mean? | In the middle of a game | With a scratch on it | *From the very start | At the very end
exam: In the formal voice, «with effect from 1 May» most likely means | *starting on 1 May | a word meaning May | 1 May is a holiday | ending on 1 May
